Subject: [PATCH 1/6] WP-0.7, enforcement (cld-advisor's series 3, patch 1;
 built on b80c68991 with series 2 applied): A GATE READS ALL IT IS ABOUT --
 THE FRAMING GATE (the seat's planted case PV21). The gate read three kinds of
 file, .md, .py and .txt, and compared letter for letter: a banned phrase in a
 .tex file under papers/ passed, and so did one in capitals on the front page.
 Review 38's own audit of the gate said so in August (B887), in a .json the
 gate did not read. THE RULE, which the patches after this one share: a gate
 reads the whole of what it is about; what it does not read it counts in its
 sentence; and what stood on the day the rule came is held by name. HERE: the
 gate reads every file in its folders that is text, whatever its name ends in,
 and compares as a reader does: in one letter case, a phrase broken over two
 lines as the phrase, a typesetter's apostrophe as an apostrophe, a word in
 emphasis as the word. Not read, and counted in the sentence: a file that is
 not text, and a file above 5 MB, which is data and not a page (the largest
 page is 2.4 MB). On main the wider reading finds one thing, as main measured:
 B887's report, which quotes a banned phrase to say what the gate misses; it
 joins the gate's exemptions by name. The gate takes 2.5 s where it took 0.4.
 The lock: tests/test_a_gate_reads_all_it_is_about.py, seven tests, each in a
 small repository of its own; on the commit before, six fail, four of them
 because the gate passes, and the control holds. 0 of 19.

